From 8af368b973cf366890f2630dc4cf0ca912387491 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 02:50:07 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(desktop): boot waits out a remote's own update
 instead of parking on "Hermes couldn't start"
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Observed on laptop-1 2026-11-13 02:33 PST: hermes-hub ran its own `hermes update`, the SSH preflight refused
startup ("Remote Hermes update process 1779396 is still running; SSH startup is paused."), and the renderer
showed the full-window "Hermes couldn't start" overlay. The update pid was gone by 02:34:44, yet the overlay
was still up at 02:36: nothing re-ran boot, so David had to press Retry.

Five whys:
1. Why did the overlay stay? boot() caught the refusal and called failDesktopBoot(); nothing schedules
   another boot after that.
2. Why no automatic retry? The bounded boot retry (#82679) only fires when main's boot snapshot says
   retryable:true or the renderer was dialing the socket. The startup route goes through
   hermes:connection:for (connectDesktopProfileRoute), which never updates the boot snapshot, so it read
   retryable:false. Even when it is retryable, 5 attempts span about a minute, shorter than an update.
3. Why does main's own update gate not recover it? RemoteUpdatePause only probes when a caller arrives;
   once the renderer gave up, no caller came.
4. Why wasn't it caught? No test drove boot through a remote-update refusal; the gate's tests cover main's
   dial dedup only.
5. Root gap: the renderer treated "remote is updating" (a condition that ends on its own) like a generic
   boot failure with a short retry budget.

Fix: the renderer recognises the refusal by the phrase every producer shares ("SSH startup is paused";
the error crosses IPC as text, so `kind`/`code` are lost) and keeps polling every 5 s outside the bounded
budget, showing "Waiting for the remote Hermes update to finish…" instead of the overlay. Main's gate
answers polls from its cached refusal without dialing until its own backoff (≤30 s) opens, so the poll costs
one IPC round-trip. A pause longer than 30 min still ends on the recovery overlay.

Tests:
- use-gateway-boot-remote-update.test.tsx: three minutes of refusals (past the 5-attempt budget, snapshot
  retryable:false) keep the overlay down with no notifyError; once the update exits, the next poll connects
  and boot completes with no click (RED on main: the overlay shows on the first refusal). Past the cap the
  overlay still shows.
- remote-update-wait.test.ts: matcher accepts the IPC-wrapped and cached forms and rejects other failures;
  every electron producer of the refusal (remote-lifecycle, windows-remote-lifecycle, remote-update-pause)
  still uses the matched phrase; the wait cap and reset.
